1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,310
In this simulated town, on average, three quarters of a household's neighbors match its color.

2
00:00:06,690 --> 00:00:09,260
How much matching do these households demand?

3
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:12,230
Just a third.

4
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:16,150
Each household counts the occupied squares around it.

5
00:00:16,530 --> 00:00:19,310
If fewer than a third of them match, it moves.

6
00:00:19,690 --> 00:00:22,130
It is happy to be outnumbered two to one.

7
00:00:22,466 --> 00:00:27,436
We read maps of real cities the same way, guessing motives from patterns.

8
00:00:27,816 --> 00:00:31,876
So how does a demand of a third build a town of three quarters?

9
00:00:32,633 --> 00:00:35,073
Rewind to the start, a random mix.

10
00:00:35,453 --> 00:00:40,433
Here, on average, half of a household's neighbors match, well above a third.

11
00:00:40,566 --> 00:00:45,116
Still, these two hundred and thirty-eight are unhappy, about one in six.

12
00:00:45,466 --> 00:00:51,486
The obvious plan is to move each of them to a random empty square where it would be content.

13
00:00:51,686 --> 00:00:55,256
Then everyone is content, and the town stays mixed.

14
00:00:55,600 --> 00:01:00,810
Round one takes two hundred moves; the other thirty-eight were content by their turn.

15
00:01:01,166 --> 00:01:04,276
If the plan had worked, this counter would read zero.

16
00:01:04,656 --> 00:01:09,716
It reads seventy-four, and sixty-seven of these were content before round one.

17
00:01:09,866 --> 00:01:11,066
They never moved.

18
00:01:11,446 --> 00:01:12,676
Their neighbors did.

19
00:01:13,433 --> 00:01:14,733
Replay one corner.

20
00:01:15,033 --> 00:01:19,683
Before round one, this gold household had three gold neighbors out of eight.

21
00:01:20,063 --> 00:01:22,913
Three eighths is more than a third, so it stayed.

22
00:01:23,266 --> 00:01:24,976
Then this neighbor moved away.

23
00:01:25,356 --> 00:01:26,816
Is it still content?

24
00:01:28,366 --> 00:01:30,096
Two gold out of seven.

25
00:01:30,476 --> 00:01:34,386
Two sevenths is less than a third, so now it wants out.

26
00:01:34,733 --> 00:01:37,193
Its neighbor here also has three eighths.

27
00:01:37,573 --> 00:01:42,133
In round two, our household leaves, and this one drops to two sevenths.

28
00:01:43,233 --> 00:01:46,933
Then the next one, also at three eighths, tips in round three.

29
00:01:48,333 --> 00:01:49,803
Now run the whole town.

30
00:01:51,603 --> 00:01:54,293
After round seven, nobody wants to move.

31
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:58,320
That whole town is one point on this chart.

32
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:04,770
Demand a quarter, and the same starting town barely sorts: fifty-eight percent.

33
00:02:05,150 --> 00:02:08,180
Demand a third, and it jumps to seventy-six.

34
00:02:08,333 --> 00:02:14,053
To see why that happens, sort the random start by each household's share of matching neighbors.

35
00:02:14,433 --> 00:02:19,003
With at most eight neighbors, these are the only shares up to sixty percent.

36
00:02:19,366 --> 00:02:28,446
At a third, everyone left of it is unhappy, two hundred and thirty-eight, including the neighbor that started our corner's chain, at two sevenths.

37
00:02:28,866 --> 00:02:32,916
Every household at three eighths is one lost match away from it.

38
00:02:33,233 --> 00:02:35,883
At a quarter, how many would start unhappy?

39
00:02:37,333 --> 00:02:45,143
Only these two bars change sides, and they're tall: now just ninety-five start unhappy, and that chain never starts.

40
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:49,620
In his 1971 paper, Thomas Schelling ran this model.

41
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:59,470
His households moved to the nearest square that suited them, on emptier boards, and there the jump came later, between about a third and a half.

42
00:03:00,533 --> 00:03:04,043
Two simulated towns, both seventy-six percent.

43
00:03:04,423 --> 00:03:07,243
In one town, households demand a third.

44
00:03:07,623 --> 00:03:10,453
In the other, nobody minds their neighbors at all.

45
00:03:10,833 --> 00:03:12,463
Which one wanted a third?

46
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:15,130
This one.

47
00:03:15,330 --> 00:03:18,980
The other was placed by a rule, with no preferences at all.

48
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:27,830
In real cities, one study finds that 1930s federal lending maps increased racial segregation for decades.

49
00:03:28,210 --> 00:03:31,930
It traces this to reduced credit in the areas marked in red.

50
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:34,810
Look again at the first town.

51
00:03:35,190 --> 00:03:41,590
As Schelling argued, whatever you guessed its people demanded, the map alone could not have told you.

